WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.669
- I've actually talked to
some of the energy folks

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:04.104
from Massachusetts
point blank on this,

00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:05.739
out of their state legislature.

00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:09.275
And their goal is to
make Massachusetts

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.444
more energy renewable friendly

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:14.848
by importing renewable
energy from other states.

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.449
So my statement to them is,

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:17.717
so what you want us to do

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.786
is put up more wind
towers in our state

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:22.489
because you don't wanna have
wind towers in your state,

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:24.924 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then you stop using
these other forms of energy,

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:28.461
but make us cover our whole
landscape with wind towers

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:30.030
so you don't have
to look at them.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.265
That's really what their plan
is, at the end of the day.

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:35.068
When I talk to them about
switching from, let's say,

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:38.138 align:start size:97% position:3%
home heating oil to burning
cleaner natural gas,

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:39.572
they'll say, well,
we're trying to just

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
get rid of all carbons
entirely, but in the meantime,

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.544
our plane is for decades
and decades and decades

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:46.146
to keep using home heating oil.

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:49.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're not really serious
about all these issues.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:51.451
They're just trying to be
able to make everyone think

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:52.652
that they really are,

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:53.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
but look at what
they're really doing,

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.588 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that makes a big
difference there.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.124
(bright music)

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:05.765
- I'm Dave Rubin and joining
me today is the junior senator

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:08.701
from the great state of
Oklahoma, James Lankford.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:11.638 align:start size:94% position:6%
Senator Lankford, welcome
to The Rubin Report.

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:13.106
- Hey, very good to see you.

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:14.340 align:start size:94% position:6%
Good to be on with you
and have the conversation.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.875
- I'm looking forward
to chatting with you

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.811 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I mentioned to you
right before we started

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:20.213 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we had your
Governor Kevin Stitt on

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.948
about a month and a half ago.

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:23.950
And I'm gonna start with
the same exact question

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.519
because I don't think people
know a lot about Oklahoma.

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:29.622
So if someone says
to you, what's going
on over in Oklahoma,

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:30.657
what do you tell 'em?

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:32.792
- Freedom and liberty,

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.260
that's what's happening
in Oklahoma, actually.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.895
It's four million great people

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:39.632 align:start size:88% position:13%
who skip away from
tornadoes occasionally.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.101
We get a bad rap for that,

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:43.269
but there's lots of
job opportunities,

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:45.705
there's lots of people
working that are there.

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:47.040
It's a really great community

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.576
and I have people that
I talk to all the time

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:53.113
that are from other places in
the country and they moved.

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:56.249
I will never move away because
of the people that are here.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so it's a pretty
remarkable state.

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:02.489
It's a left coast
flyover territory

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:03.923
but we're glad they
just fly over us

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause it's a great
place to be able to live.

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:07.861
- What kind of influx
are you guys getting

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:08.928
in these last two years?

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:10.396
You know, everyone talks about

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:13.233
all the Cali and New York
people going to Texas,

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:14.767
going to Florida,
going to Tennessee,

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.669
but you guys are getting
a pretty decent influx

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:17.504
as well, right?

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:18.738
- We are actually.

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:21.808
I talked to real estate
agents around Oklahoma

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:23.042
and I'll always ask them,

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:24.577
"Who are you selling
houses to right now?"

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:26.246 align:start size:97% position:3%
And they're telling me they're
selling houses to people

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:29.482
from Washington state, from
Oregon, from California,

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
from Minnesota, from
all these states

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:33.286
are moving into Oklahoma.

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:35.889
And my first comment to them
is, "Are you checking voter ID

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:37.590
with them before you
sell 'em my house?"

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.058
- Right.

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:40.260
- Which by the way, they
can't do, that's not legal.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.529 align:start size:88% position:13%
But then the second
statement they make is,

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:45.198
oh no, these are the people
we want to be able to move.

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:47.066
They're fleeing from all of that

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:48.935
in those areas of the country

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:50.803
and they're coming to
a place that's more-

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:53.139 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Do you think people are
getting it because, you know,

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:54.774
there always is that concern.

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:56.809
I've told the story on
the show many times,

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.578
when I moved to
Florida last year

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:01.080
and I'll be walking my dog
and people would come up

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.782 align:start size:88% position:13%
and say, "Hi, oh you're
the new neighbor."

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:04.017 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they'd say,
"Where are you from?"

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:05.919
I'd say, "Cali", they'd
freak out for a second

00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:08.955
and then, you know, I'd have
a Florida man shirt or hat

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:11.157
or I'd show a picture of me
and the governor or something

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:12.692
and then they're okay.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:15.028
But the OGs there is
that concern, right?

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:17.030
That you guys have done
it right in Oklahoma

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.998 align:start size:81% position:19%
and you don't want
that mucked up.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:20.233
- That's correct.

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:21.467
And again, we're open
to everybody coming.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:22.902
All political persuasions.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
People talk about Oklahoma
as a very red state.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:28.141 align:start size:81% position:19%
All 77 counties
voted a Republican

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:29.642 align:start size:88% position:13%
the last three
presidential elections.

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we get that
reputation somewhat,

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:33.646
but we've got lots of people

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.682
and lots of diversity
of opinion on that.

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:37.283
And that's a good thing for us

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:38.785
to continue to be
able to drive that.

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:40.053
But we also wanna be able
to maintain the culture

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:42.121 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we have, of
freedom, of opportunity,

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.490 align:start size:84% position:16%
of business starts,
of low taxes.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.392
I mean those are all things
that we want to continue

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.394
to be able to maintain
and protect in our state.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:51.064 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And you guys must be
pretty proud of the policies

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:53.933
when it came to Covid and
not crushing the economy

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:57.637 align:start size:97% position:3%
and killing all the schools
and all of that stuff.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:01.174 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yep. Schools stayed
open, churches stayed open,

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:02.375
businesses stayed open.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:05.144 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we had a few week
time period there

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:07.447
at the very beginning, in 2020,

00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:09.282
where things were closed down
like the rest of the country

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:11.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then reopened
rapidly only to say,

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:13.019
hey, this is a terrible idea.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:14.320
And got things back open again.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.155 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it showed the
real difference there

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:17.557 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the way our
businesses bounced back.

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:19.225
- Are you shocked how different

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:20.827
the blue and red
states have become?

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:23.363 align:start position:0%
I mean obviously there's always
been a difference in policy

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:25.231
between Democrats
and Republicans,

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:26.599
but how the last couple years

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:30.136 align:start position:0%
have really unearthed something
that is causing people

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:33.306
to flee certain states and
go to certain other states

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:34.907 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it really doesn't
go the other way.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm guessing you're not
losing a lot of Oklahomans

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:38.444
who are on their way to Cali.

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.079
- No.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:42.315 align:start position:0%
No, we don't have a lot of
Oklahomans that are packing up.

00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:43.750
In fact, if you want to-

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:44.917
This is kind of the running joke

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:46.219
that people have in Oklahoma.

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:48.721 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you want to rent
a u-Haul truck,

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:51.591
a one-way rental to California,

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:53.826
you could almost
get that for free.

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:56.996 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because it's so cheap
to go towards California

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.432
with a one-way truck, but
if you're coming out of it,

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:01.901
it's really expensive to be
able to come the other way

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.903 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they're looking
for more people to-

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:05.338
So no, we're not surprised.

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:06.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are very few people
that leave Oklahoma,

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:08.374
they come to Oklahoma.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:09.475
We're grateful to be able
to have great families.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:11.311
We have a lot of veterans that,

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:14.480 align:start size:97% position:3%
as they serve at Tinker
Air Force Base or Fort Sill

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.684 align:start size:94% position:6%
or all kinds of places,
or Enid, around our state.

00:05:17.684 --> 00:05:19.686 align:start size:88% position:13%
They'll serve there
and then 10 years later

00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:20.920
they'll circle back to Oklahoma

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.855
and retire coming
back to Oklahoma

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.957 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they just love
the patriotic environment.

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.326 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I don't know if
you've seen the meme,

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:28.628 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there's a really
hilarious meme of Gavin Newsom

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:30.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
working at U-Haul, number
one U-Haul salesman

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:33.199
of the last three
years, I think.

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:37.503 align:start size:97% position:3%
- He is. He's a good exporter
of things California,

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
because people are
leaving his state, so.

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:40.940
- Yeah, exactly.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.209
Can you talk a little bit
about just the economy

00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:45.945
and what you guys are doing
on the energy side of things?

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:47.480
'Cause that's the type
of thing that I think

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.982
sort of translates to people
that don't live in Oklahoma

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.951
that are watching right now.

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:54.020
- Yeah. One of the
lowest unemployment
rates in the country

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.221
is in Oklahoma.

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:57.790 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we have very high
employment in our state.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:58.991
We are an energy state,

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:01.127
but we are truly all
the above energy state.

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:02.695
People know us as an
oil and gas state,

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:04.230
but we do oil and gas.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.866
We do a little bit of coal in
the eastern side of our state.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:08.601
We have a lot of wind power.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:09.902
Sing the song Oklahoma,

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:11.437 align:start size:88% position:13%
where the wind comes
sweeping down the plane.

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:12.972
We've taken that wind

00:06:12.972 --> 00:06:14.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we've harnessed it
for power and electricity.

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:16.175
We use it not only in our state,

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:17.944
but we export it to
other states as well.

00:06:17.944 --> 00:06:19.445
We have solar power,

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:22.281
we have hydroelectric in the
eastern part of our state.

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:23.483
We have geothermal.

00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:25.218
We're an all the
above energy state.

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:26.419
And it's always funny to me,

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.454 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'll have some of my
liberal colleagues in DC

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:31.124
start lecturing me about oil
and gas and I'll say to them,

00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:34.727 align:start size:97% position:3%
let's look up right now your
portfolio of energy usage

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.196 align:start size:88% position:13%
in your state and let's
compare it to mine.

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:39.766
I'd be glad to be able to
lay Oklahoma side by side

00:06:39.766 --> 00:06:41.267
with Massachusetts for instance,

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:44.470 align:start size:97% position:3%
because we're using clean
natural gas to heat our homes.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:46.005
Home heating oil
in their basement,

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:48.508
which might as well be
burning a lump of coal

00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:51.344
in their basement, to be able
to heat their homes on it.

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:52.678
There's no comparison on it.

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:54.847
We're so much cleaner in
our energy development

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:57.683
than the northeast is, but yet
they're always the one trying

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:59.485
to be able to pile in
new requirements on us.

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:01.721 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we've already done
that in our state

00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:03.523
and we're very proud to
have so many energy jobs

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:05.391
and such great energy diversity.

00:07:05.391 --> 00:07:07.860 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So when you're
talking to a colleague,

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.796
let's say a senator
in Massachusetts,

00:07:09.796 --> 00:07:12.598 align:start size:97% position:3%
maybe Elizabeth Warren, just
using her as an example,

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.333
and you say, hey, you know,
we're doing it right here

00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:16.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we've got this
diversification of energy.

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:18.204
You guys don't have that yet.

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:20.773 align:start size:94% position:6%
You seem to want to
destroy the energy sector.

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:24.110
Do they respond? Are you able
to have that conversation?

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.612 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do they realize how
misguided the policies are?

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:28.681
- Well they don't like the
comparison side by side,

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:30.416
but I've actually talked
to some of the energy folks

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:32.485
from Massachusetts
point blank on this,

00:07:32.485 --> 00:07:34.454
out of their state legislature.

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:37.490
And their goal is to
make Massachusetts

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:40.159
more energy renewable friendly

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:43.529
by importing renewable
energy from other states.

00:07:43.529 --> 00:07:46.299
So my statement to them is,
so what you want us to do

00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:48.000
is put up more wind
towers in our state

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
because you don't want to
have wind towers in your state

00:07:50.837 --> 00:07:53.639 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then you stop using
these other forms of energy

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:57.143
but make us cover our whole
landscape with wind towers

00:07:57.143 --> 00:07:58.778
so you don't have
to look at 'em.

00:07:58.778 --> 00:08:00.913
That's really what their plan
is, at the end of the day.

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:03.182
When I talk to them
about switching from,

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:06.719
let's say, home heating oil to
burning cleaner natural gas,

00:08:06.719 --> 00:08:08.287
they'll say, well, we're
trying to just get rid of

00:08:08.287 --> 00:08:11.657
all carbons entirely, but
in the meantime our plan is

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:13.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
for decades and
decades and decades

00:08:13.125 --> 00:08:14.727
to keep using home heating oil.

00:08:14.727 --> 00:08:17.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're not really serious
about all these issues.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.066
They're just trying to be
able to make everyone think

00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:21.968
that they really are, but look
at what they're really doing

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:24.437 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that makes a big
difference there.

00:08:24.437 --> 00:08:27.073 align:start size:97% position:3%
- What kind of tension do
you have as a state that has,

00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:28.674
you know, you've
got the renewables,

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:32.278
you've got the traditional
energy sources, you know.

00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:34.280
The federal government
obviously wants you to go

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:35.515
in one direction.

00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:36.449
You don't wanna put
people outta work.

00:08:36.449 --> 00:08:37.850
How do you manage all that?

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.318
- Well, a couple things in that.

00:08:39.318 --> 00:08:40.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have a lot of
manufacturing in the state

00:08:40.887 --> 00:08:43.389
'cause we have some of the
lowest electricity prices

00:08:43.389 --> 00:08:44.724
in the country.

00:08:44.724 --> 00:08:46.526 align:start position:0%
So we literally have
manufacturers moving to Oklahoma

00:08:46.526 --> 00:08:47.927
for cheaper electricity

00:08:47.927 --> 00:08:49.962
because we produce
so much electricity

00:08:49.962 --> 00:08:52.064
from such a diverse
portfolio of it.

00:08:52.064 --> 00:08:53.566
So that makes a big
difference on it.

00:08:53.566 --> 00:08:55.601
The other parts are the
jobs that go with that

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:57.670
'cause we'll have a lot
of jobs that are created

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:01.040 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the energy market and in
manufacturing because of that.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.775
That's why we have such
a low unemployment rate

00:09:02.775 --> 00:09:04.610
in our state.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:07.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it does make a difference
in our own economy.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.782 align:start size:97% position:3%
- One of the things that I
talked to Governor Stitt about

00:09:09.782 --> 00:09:11.350
that he was very fired up about,

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:14.387
really wanted to focus on, was
some of the tensions existing

00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:16.789 align:start position:0%
around what's going on with
the Native American communities

00:09:16.789 --> 00:09:19.325
and law disparity and
all of this stuff.

00:09:19.325 --> 00:09:22.261 align:start size:84% position:16%
How is that all
shaking out right now

00:09:22.261 --> 00:09:24.263
and your position
on all of that?

00:09:24.263 --> 00:09:25.765
- So yeah, we've
got a pretty wide

00:09:25.765 --> 00:09:26.699
diversity of opinion on that.

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:28.200
We have 4 million Oklahomans.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:30.803
About 7% of our state
is Native American.

00:09:30.803 --> 00:09:34.140
It's a very high percentage
for any other state on it.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.944
We now have six tribes that
have a special allotment

00:09:37.944 --> 00:09:40.179 align:start size:97% position:3%
from the Supreme Court
called the McGirt decision.

00:09:40.179 --> 00:09:43.783
That McGirt decision said
that the federal government

00:09:43.783 --> 00:09:46.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
has to be the one to actually
prosecute major crimes

00:09:46.419 --> 00:09:48.721 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the state for those
particular tribes

00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:49.989
and in those tribal areas.

00:09:49.989 --> 00:09:51.824 align:start size:81% position:19%
It's not small and
a lot of people

00:09:51.824 --> 00:09:53.359
across the country
may not track this.

00:09:53.359 --> 00:09:56.128 align:start size:97% position:3%
They may see Indian
reservations in other states

00:09:56.128 --> 00:09:58.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
where it's only tribal
members live in that area.

00:09:58.064 --> 00:09:59.498
That's not so in Oklahoma.

00:09:59.498 --> 00:10:01.334
Oklahoma we're very
integrated on it.

00:10:01.334 --> 00:10:02.902
You would literally
drive through areas

00:10:02.902 --> 00:10:05.104
and not have any idea
if this is tribal area

00:10:05.104 --> 00:10:06.572
or non-tribal area.

00:10:06.572 --> 00:10:09.041
Or you may have one house
that's a tribal member

00:10:09.041 --> 00:10:12.111
and next door to them is
a non-tribal member on it.

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:15.047 align:start position:0%
Because of just our integration
of where we are as a state.

00:10:15.047 --> 00:10:17.383 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is dramatically
shifted what's happening

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:18.985
in law enforcement in our state

00:10:18.985 --> 00:10:21.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
where a police
officer doesn't know

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.756
whether they can
prosecute a crime

00:10:23.756 --> 00:10:26.192
or there has to be the FBI
coming to be able to do it

00:10:26.192 --> 00:10:28.928
and it's created quite
a bit of chaos and stir.

00:10:28.928 --> 00:10:31.664 align:start size:97% position:3%
While the tribes are trying
to step up and do some things,

00:10:31.664 --> 00:10:34.100
we have unequal justice
currently in our state

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.935 align:start size:88% position:13%
and this is something
that has to be resolved.

00:10:35.935 --> 00:10:37.803 align:start size:88% position:13%
We're gonna have to
have a federal solution,

00:10:37.803 --> 00:10:39.772
but it's gonna have to have
all of those tribes agree,

00:10:39.772 --> 00:10:42.108 align:start size:84% position:16%
our state agree and
the federal delegation

00:10:42.108 --> 00:10:43.643
to also be able to agree on this

00:10:43.643 --> 00:10:45.344
to be able to get
some solutions.

00:10:45.344 --> 00:10:47.380 align:start size:84% position:16%
In the meantime,
we'll have individuals

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:49.348
and we've already
had an occasion

00:10:49.348 --> 00:10:51.817
where an individual
had multiple DUIs

00:10:51.817 --> 00:10:54.754
but was let off
every single time

00:10:54.754 --> 00:10:56.989
and then that same
person runs over

00:10:56.989 --> 00:10:58.257
and murders a five-year old.

00:10:58.257 --> 00:11:00.726 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's no way to
explain to some dad,

00:11:00.726 --> 00:11:04.630 align:start position:0%
hey, it's because this
particular individual was tribal

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:05.998
and so they can get off with it

00:11:05.998 --> 00:11:07.400
when a non-tribal member cannot.

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:09.035
That can't be so.

00:11:09.035 --> 00:11:11.237
We've gotta be able to have
equal justice and process

00:11:11.237 --> 00:11:13.806
and we've gotta have some
way for law enforcement

00:11:13.806 --> 00:11:15.274
to be able to prosecute this.

00:11:15.274 --> 00:11:16.776 align:start size:97% position:3%
So there's something that
came out of the Supreme Court

00:11:16.776 --> 00:11:18.277
several years ago.

00:11:18.277 --> 00:11:20.212
We're still working to be able
to get to resolution on this

00:11:20.212 --> 00:11:22.682 align:start size:94% position:6%
and to keep working until
we get to resolution.

00:11:22.682 --> 00:11:25.084
- Yeah. Are you trying to get
it back to the Supreme Court?

00:11:25.084 --> 00:11:27.887
I'm actually not even sure
technically how that happens.

00:11:29.288 --> 00:11:31.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So there's actually a case
that's already come back

00:11:31.223 --> 00:11:32.525
to the Supreme Court
called the Castro-

00:11:32.525 --> 00:11:34.593
It started with McGirt
and then it went back.

00:11:34.593 --> 00:11:36.896 align:start size:97% position:3%
It was called
Castro-Huerta, second case.

00:11:36.896 --> 00:11:39.031
And then it helped
define, for instance,

00:11:39.031 --> 00:11:43.202 align:start size:97% position:3%
if a non-tribal member commits
a crime on a tribal member,

00:11:43.202 --> 00:11:44.770
where does that go?

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:46.772
Does that go to the FBI or
does that go to the state?

00:11:46.772 --> 00:11:48.307 align:start size:88% position:13%
The Supreme Court ruled
in the second case.

00:11:48.307 --> 00:11:49.642
Well that one goes to the state.

00:11:49.642 --> 00:11:51.911
So you can see the
chaos that's going on

00:11:51.911 --> 00:11:54.113 align:start size:97% position:3%
in trying to be able to
prosecute through individuals.

00:11:54.113 --> 00:11:56.749
Now you gotta know the
perpetrator of a crime

00:11:56.749 --> 00:11:59.552 align:start position:0%
before you know who can actually
go through and investigate

00:11:59.552 --> 00:12:00.820
and prosecute that crime.

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:02.254
But if you knew who
the perpetrator was,

00:12:02.254 --> 00:12:05.224 align:start position:0%
you would've prevented the
crime in the first place on it.

00:12:05.224 --> 00:12:07.460 align:start size:81% position:19%
So it's a very
difficult process.

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:09.662
So yes, it may go back
to the Supreme Court.

00:12:09.662 --> 00:12:12.965
I'm doubtful that it will
fully, the Supreme Court.

00:12:12.965 --> 00:12:15.534 align:start size:94% position:6%
What we've gotta do is
get a piece of legislation

00:12:15.534 --> 00:12:18.337 align:start size:97% position:3%
through the federal
government, through Congress,

00:12:18.337 --> 00:12:20.372
and have agreement
with the tribes,

00:12:20.372 --> 00:12:21.607
agreement with the state,

00:12:21.607 --> 00:12:24.043 align:start size:84% position:16%
and agreement with the
federal legislature

00:12:24.043 --> 00:12:26.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
to be able to resolve
these differences

00:12:26.312 --> 00:12:28.347
and try to figure out how to
be able to do prosecutions.

00:12:28.347 --> 00:12:31.317 align:start size:97% position:3%
In the meantime, all of
our courts are overwhelmed.

00:12:31.317 --> 00:12:33.385 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't know if you track
all this, but federal courts

00:12:33.385 --> 00:12:35.554 align:start size:97% position:3%
don't typically handle
murder cases, for instance.

00:12:35.554 --> 00:12:38.724
That's a pretty rare thing
for a federal jurisdiction

00:12:38.724 --> 00:12:39.558
to handle a murder case.

00:12:39.558 --> 00:12:40.993
That's a state crime.

00:12:40.993 --> 00:12:45.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
57% of all the federal
murder cases in the country

00:12:45.164 --> 00:12:48.167
are in one small part
of southeast Oklahoma.

00:12:48.167 --> 00:12:52.271
57% in the whole country
of the federal murder cases

00:12:52.271 --> 00:12:53.806
are just in that
one small section.

00:12:53.806 --> 00:12:56.308 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's not the
federal jurisdiction.

00:12:56.308 --> 00:12:57.877 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's not what they
handle typically.

00:12:57.877 --> 00:13:00.012 align:start size:97% position:3%
They don't have the
expertise to be able to do it

00:13:00.012 --> 00:13:01.480
and they're overwhelmed with it.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.116
And we've got occasions
where if a car is still-

00:13:04.116 --> 00:13:05.651
- Right, just to
be clear though,

00:13:05.651 --> 00:13:06.585
it's not because you have
a crazy amount of murders,

00:13:06.585 --> 00:13:08.554
it's because of this decision.

00:13:08.554 --> 00:13:09.789
- That's correct.

00:13:09.789 --> 00:13:11.023
It's because the
federal government

00:13:11.023 --> 00:13:12.591
typically doesn't have
that many murder cases.

00:13:12.591 --> 00:13:14.059
So all that they have,

00:13:14.059 --> 00:13:16.495 align:start size:97% position:3%
the vast majority of the
cases that they have at all,

00:13:16.495 --> 00:13:18.998
are in Oklahoma because
of this McGirt decision.

00:13:18.998 --> 00:13:20.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if they're prosecuting
those murder cases,

00:13:20.466 --> 00:13:23.702 align:start size:97% position:3%
rightfully so, that also
means if someone stole a car

00:13:23.702 --> 00:13:26.205
from a tribal member, no
one's prosecuting 'em.

00:13:26.205 --> 00:13:28.340
And so now they're
just victims of crime

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.975
that no one's prosecuting

00:13:29.975 --> 00:13:32.144
'cause no one has the
jurisdiction or the
time to go get it.

00:13:32.144 --> 00:13:33.679
That can't be so.

00:13:33.679 --> 00:13:36.148
This is why it's so important
that our state and our tribes

00:13:36.148 --> 00:13:37.416 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the federal
government work together

00:13:37.416 --> 00:13:38.684
to be able to resolve this

00:13:38.684 --> 00:13:40.152
because we've gotta
have equal justice

00:13:40.152 --> 00:13:41.487
for every single person.

00:13:41.487 --> 00:13:43.422 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're not at at odds
with each other,

00:13:43.422 --> 00:13:45.958 align:start size:84% position:16%
the tribes and the
state and individuals.

00:13:45.958 --> 00:13:47.626
We're 4 million neighbors.

00:13:47.626 --> 00:13:49.195 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've gotta just
figure this part out,

00:13:49.195 --> 00:13:50.429
but it's a mess right now.

00:13:50.429 --> 00:13:51.530
- Yeah, it really
sounds like it.

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:52.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean equal protection
under the law.

00:13:52.998 --> 00:13:56.535 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's one of the most am
American ideas that there is

00:13:56.535 --> 00:13:58.037
and hopefully it works out.

00:13:58.037 --> 00:14:00.773
Let me ask you a little bit
about just DC in general

00:14:00.773 --> 00:14:03.776
and being a senator,
having to go to DC,

00:14:03.776 --> 00:14:05.511
which is obviously
very, very different

00:14:05.511 --> 00:14:08.514 align:start size:84% position:16%
than when you're back
home in Oklahoma.

00:14:08.514 --> 00:14:10.149
The red wave didn't happen,

00:14:10.149 --> 00:14:12.518 align:start size:97% position:3%
so you probably thought maybe
you were gonna, you know,

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:13.652
have a little more power.

00:14:13.652 --> 00:14:15.521
The Republicans, at this point,

00:14:15.521 --> 00:14:19.225
like what's the general state
of things in DC right now?

00:14:20.659 --> 00:14:22.261
- Well right now, it's a mess

00:14:22.261 --> 00:14:24.763 align:start position:0%
because of what's going on with
the appropriations process.

00:14:24.763 --> 00:14:27.967
And you're right, we
all expected the red
wave would happen.

00:14:27.967 --> 00:14:31.203
We hoped for that, prayed
for that, quite frankly.

00:14:31.203 --> 00:14:32.438
That didn't happen

00:14:32.438 --> 00:14:34.640
and we're still going
through all the process

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.475
of the whys and the
whats of all of that,

00:14:36.475 --> 00:14:38.077 align:start size:84% position:16%
but that didn't happen
around the country.

00:14:38.077 --> 00:14:41.013
So it's not just praying for
me a little more power on it.

00:14:41.013 --> 00:14:43.315 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's a little bit of
sanity into the process.

00:14:43.315 --> 00:14:46.051
You realize in the
Senate there's been
no oversight hearings

00:14:46.051 --> 00:14:47.519
on immigration.

00:14:47.519 --> 00:14:50.289 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've got 2 million people
illegally crossing the boarder

00:14:50.289 --> 00:14:51.223
in the past year.

00:14:51.223 --> 00:14:52.458
It's about to get even worse

00:14:52.458 --> 00:14:54.193
and the senate's not
even holding hearings.

00:14:54.193 --> 00:14:57.029
Like don't even look at
that, just ignore it.

00:14:57.029 --> 00:14:58.797
That's crazy to be
able to have that.

00:14:58.797 --> 00:15:00.366 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so we want to
be able to get that.

00:15:00.366 --> 00:15:01.934 align:start size:84% position:16%
All the fiscal
sanity that we need

00:15:01.934 --> 00:15:04.803
to be able to bring back
into the dialogue right now

00:15:04.803 --> 00:15:07.006
of how we're gonna handle
the appropriations process,

00:15:07.006 --> 00:15:08.974 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is in a
shambles right now.

00:15:08.974 --> 00:15:10.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or even how we're
gonna handle spending.

00:15:10.476 --> 00:15:12.077
Spending continues
to be able to go up.

00:15:12.077 --> 00:15:14.213
Democrats don't want
to even discuss that,

00:15:14.213 --> 00:15:15.481
have hearings on it,

00:15:15.481 --> 00:15:17.383
to be able to talk long
term about entitlements.

00:15:17.383 --> 00:15:19.785 align:start size:97% position:3%
What's gonna happen to be
able to stabilize our spending

00:15:19.785 --> 00:15:21.921
or to get us back into
balance, no dialogue.

00:15:21.921 --> 00:15:24.456 align:start size:97% position:3%
So for Republicans to come
back in the lead in the Senate

00:15:24.456 --> 00:15:26.091 align:start size:97% position:3%
was really a shift in
leadership in the committees

00:15:26.091 --> 00:15:29.261
and an opportunity to bring
some hearings up and to say,

00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:30.529
let's talk about
debt and deficit,

00:15:30.529 --> 00:15:32.031
let's talk about immigration,

00:15:32.031 --> 00:15:33.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
how to be able to
solve these problems.

00:15:33.832 --> 00:15:36.335 align:start position:0%
Let's talk about how we're
gonna handle complicated issues

00:15:36.335 --> 00:15:38.704 align:start position:0%
that are currently being ignored
that are really important

00:15:38.704 --> 00:15:40.673 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the American people,
like the economy,

00:15:40.673 --> 00:15:42.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
and how we're gonna actually
bring down inflation.

00:15:42.541 --> 00:15:45.778 align:start size:94% position:6%
But instead it's just move
along and ignore that.

00:15:45.778 --> 00:15:47.246 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's the frustrating
part about that.

00:15:47.246 --> 00:15:49.949 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Do you think that if the
Republicans had got the Senate

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:52.184
that they would've
actually accomplished

00:15:52.184 --> 00:15:53.619
some of the things that
you're talking about?

00:15:53.619 --> 00:15:56.088 align:start position:0%
I think one of the frustrations
that a lot of people have

00:15:56.088 --> 00:15:58.857
that care about politics
and pay attention to this.

00:15:58.857 --> 00:16:00.392 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think there's a
feeling sort of like,

00:16:00.392 --> 00:16:03.095 align:start size:97% position:3%
certainly amongst my
audience and me personally,

00:16:03.095 --> 00:16:05.230 align:start size:84% position:16%
like the Republicans
are obviously

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:06.498
way better than the Democrats.

00:16:06.498 --> 00:16:07.967
The Democrats have
lost their mind,

00:16:07.967 --> 00:16:10.602
but like you shouldn't expect
a lot from the Republicans

00:16:10.602 --> 00:16:12.237
and they're probably
gonna let you down.

00:16:12.237 --> 00:16:13.939
Do you think you guys
would've had a chance

00:16:13.939 --> 00:16:16.475
to actually do some things
or do you think, you know,

00:16:16.475 --> 00:16:17.676
the Republicans
do have Congress.

00:16:17.676 --> 00:16:19.211
You think they're gonna
get some stuff done?

00:16:19.211 --> 00:16:21.113
- Yeah, so there's a
couple things there.

00:16:21.113 --> 00:16:22.481
If we'd have won
the Senate back,

00:16:22.481 --> 00:16:24.116 align:start size:97% position:3%
one, we could have
controlled Biden's nominees.

00:16:24.116 --> 00:16:27.619 align:start size:97% position:3%
He's putting in really
radical progressive nominees.

00:16:27.619 --> 00:16:29.321
We have been able to
block a few of those,

00:16:29.321 --> 00:16:32.091 align:start size:88% position:13%
but now they'll be no
way to block any of 'em.

00:16:32.091 --> 00:16:35.027
He'll have 51 votes and he
will be able to rapidly move

00:16:35.027 --> 00:16:36.562
all these nominations through.

00:16:36.562 --> 00:16:38.731
So yes, we could have done
that and controlled that.

00:16:38.731 --> 00:16:40.299
We could have managed
the different issues

00:16:40.299 --> 00:16:41.967
that are coming up on hearings.

00:16:41.967 --> 00:16:43.936 align:start size:94% position:6%
We still have to deal with
a Democrat White House.

00:16:43.936 --> 00:16:45.838
And so just passing everything

00:16:45.838 --> 00:16:47.139
and saying that we're
gonna get that passed

00:16:47.139 --> 00:16:48.874
to Democrat White
House is not true.

00:16:48.874 --> 00:16:50.376
But it gives us a
lot more leverage.

00:16:50.376 --> 00:16:51.610
And if you wanna talk
about a big issue,

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:54.713 align:start size:84% position:16%
the 2017 tax policy
that happened

00:16:54.713 --> 00:16:56.548
when we did the tax
cut in Jobs Act,

00:16:56.548 --> 00:16:59.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
some of those areas
expire in 2026.

00:16:59.451 --> 00:17:00.953
So in the next two years,

00:17:00.953 --> 00:17:03.122
we're gonna be doing
major tax negotiations.

00:17:03.122 --> 00:17:05.958 align:start size:97% position:3%
It would've made a world of
difference to have Republicans

00:17:05.958 --> 00:17:08.060 align:start size:84% position:16%
as the lead negotiator
on that tax piece

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:10.462
than it's gonna be
to have Democrats as
the lead negotiators

00:17:10.462 --> 00:17:11.864
on those tax areas.

00:17:11.864 --> 00:17:13.599 align:start size:97% position:3%
So yes, I do think it would've
made a big difference.

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:16.035
But by the way, not all
Republicans think alike.

00:17:16.035 --> 00:17:19.138 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's always funny, everybody
elects their Republican

00:17:19.138 --> 00:17:21.040
from their state and
they'll say to me,

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:22.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Why doesn't everybody
function like you?"

00:17:22.608 --> 00:17:24.710
And I'll say, because not
everyone lives in Oklahoma

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.078
and thinks like Oklahomans.

00:17:26.078 --> 00:17:28.514
Not everyone's as
conservatives I am.

00:17:28.514 --> 00:17:31.016 align:start position:0%
There are other states that
are not as conservative as ours

00:17:31.016 --> 00:17:33.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we have a pretty
diverse opinion about that.

00:17:33.419 --> 00:17:35.988
And republicans tend to be
able to not fly in formation.

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:37.356
They fly like eagles.

00:17:37.356 --> 00:17:39.958 align:start size:88% position:13%
They don't fly in
formation all the time.

00:17:39.958 --> 00:17:41.060
- It's like a bunch
of libertarians,

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:42.294
you get 'em around the table

00:17:42.294 --> 00:17:44.396
and they argue about the
driver's license issue

00:17:44.396 --> 00:17:47.066 align:start size:97% position:3%
for about three hours and then
everybody goes home angry.

00:17:47.066 --> 00:17:49.268
I think that's pretty
much how it works.

00:17:49.268 --> 00:17:52.671 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm wondering, as a new
Floridian, I do see this place

00:17:52.671 --> 00:17:55.641
that I'm in as sort of the
blueprint for a free America.

00:17:55.641 --> 00:17:57.810 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I know that
Governor DeSantis,

00:17:57.810 --> 00:17:59.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
actually just a few months
ago I was at an event

00:17:59.812 --> 00:18:01.346
where he got a few-

00:18:01.346 --> 00:18:02.848
about 10 governors together.

00:18:02.848 --> 00:18:05.017
That's where I met Governor
Stitt for the first time.

00:18:05.017 --> 00:18:08.554 align:start size:97% position:3%
Where they're starting to
coordinate a little bit more,

00:18:08.554 --> 00:18:10.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
sort of outside of the
federal government.

00:18:10.589 --> 00:18:13.725
And I wonder what you think
of that just conceptually

00:18:13.725 --> 00:18:16.528
that the red states are kind
of starting to do some things

00:18:16.528 --> 00:18:18.831
and not worrying always,

00:18:18.831 --> 00:18:20.232
hey what does Biden
think about this

00:18:20.232 --> 00:18:22.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
or the federal government
think about this?

00:18:23.335 --> 00:18:24.269
- Yeah, they shouldn't have to.

00:18:24.269 --> 00:18:25.537
Welcome to federalism.

00:18:25.537 --> 00:18:27.172
That's how our system
was set up, actually.

00:18:27.172 --> 00:18:30.075 align:start position:0%
Each state was supposed to
compete with the state next door

00:18:30.075 --> 00:18:32.010 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we get better
in that competition.

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:33.812
We should be doing legislation

00:18:33.812 --> 00:18:35.547
that other states look
at our state and go,

00:18:35.547 --> 00:18:36.849
wow, that's really
good legislation.

00:18:36.849 --> 00:18:39.118
We should do that in our state.

00:18:39.118 --> 00:18:41.086 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so that competition
actually helps us.

00:18:41.086 --> 00:18:43.555
For Republican governors
to be able to get together

00:18:43.555 --> 00:18:45.090 align:start size:84% position:16%
and to be able to say,
what are you doing

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.659
that's actually helping your
employment, your tax policy.

00:18:47.659 --> 00:18:49.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
What are you doing
that's being able to help

00:18:49.128 --> 00:18:50.062
in different areas?

00:18:50.062 --> 00:18:51.196
Great.

00:18:51.196 --> 00:18:52.731
Continue that idea.

00:18:52.731 --> 00:18:54.766
Our attorneys general have
done that for a long time

00:18:54.766 --> 00:18:57.035 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's been very effective
for attorneys general

00:18:57.035 --> 00:18:58.537
across the nation,

00:18:58.537 --> 00:19:00.572
to be able to swap ideas
and be able to file suits

00:19:00.572 --> 00:19:02.774 align:start size:97% position:3%
against the federal government
to be able to push back

00:19:02.774 --> 00:19:04.009
on that encroachment

00:19:04.009 --> 00:19:06.712
in a way uniquely state
attorneys general can,

00:19:06.712 --> 00:19:09.314
that I, as a senator,
can't file suit against

00:19:09.314 --> 00:19:11.350 align:start size:84% position:16%
these different issues
like a state can.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.118 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so when the states
cooperate together,

00:19:13.118 --> 00:19:14.486
it makes a big difference.

00:19:14.486 --> 00:19:15.854
And that's how our
system was set up.

00:19:15.854 --> 00:19:18.924 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I ask basically all
my guests this question.

00:19:18.924 --> 00:19:20.526
I wish I could get
more Democrats on

00:19:20.526 --> 00:19:24.563
to ask them this question,
but they don't wanna show up.

00:19:24.563 --> 00:19:26.265
We do ask.

00:19:26.265 --> 00:19:28.233 align:start size:97% position:3%
What do you think has happened
to the Democrat party?

00:19:28.233 --> 00:19:30.068
I mean this is not
the Democrat party.

00:19:30.068 --> 00:19:32.538 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was a Democrat
for much of my life.

00:19:32.538 --> 00:19:35.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is certainly not
the Democrat party of JFK

00:19:35.374 --> 00:19:37.876 align:start size:97% position:3%
or of Daniel Patrick
Moynihan, Ed Koch, et cetera.

00:19:37.876 --> 00:19:40.412 align:start size:88% position:13%
The sort of old-school,
blue dog liberal

00:19:40.412 --> 00:19:42.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you probably had a decent
amount of them in Oklahoma.

00:19:42.481 --> 00:19:46.151 align:start size:84% position:16%
It seems to be
gone at this point.

00:19:46.151 --> 00:19:48.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, the party's so
dramatically shifted on this

00:19:48.887 --> 00:19:50.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Oklahoma have
kind of a running joke

00:19:50.889 --> 00:19:52.991
that I have with folks
to say President Reagan

00:19:52.991 --> 00:19:56.161 align:start size:94% position:6%
got Democrats in Oklahoma
to vote Republican,

00:19:56.161 --> 00:19:58.463
President Obama got
Democrats in Oklahoma

00:19:58.463 --> 00:20:00.832
to re-register as a Republican.

00:20:00.832 --> 00:20:02.935 align:start size:97% position:3%
'Cause there was such a
massive shift that's happened.

00:20:02.935 --> 00:20:05.204
But if you go back
just 15 years ago,

00:20:05.204 --> 00:20:08.807 align:start position:0%
every statewide elected official
in Oklahoma was a Democrat.

00:20:08.807 --> 00:20:11.143
And that's 15 years ago.
It's not that long ago.

00:20:11.143 --> 00:20:13.478 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's been this dramatic
shift that has occurred

00:20:13.478 --> 00:20:15.347
in the National Democrat Party

00:20:15.347 --> 00:20:17.783 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you feel it in
other ways in our states

00:20:17.783 --> 00:20:19.218
with re-registration.

00:20:19.218 --> 00:20:21.420 align:start size:97% position:3%
As people that have been
lifelong Democrats have said,

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:22.988 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't even
recognize this party.

00:20:22.988 --> 00:20:24.957 align:start size:88% position:13%
And they may be
shifting to independent

00:20:24.957 --> 00:20:26.858 align:start size:88% position:13%
or they may be
shifting to Republican,

00:20:26.858 --> 00:20:28.961
but they're shifting away
from Democrats to say,

00:20:28.961 --> 00:20:31.296 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't follow these
social policies,

00:20:31.296 --> 00:20:33.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't follow this
crazy spending policy,

00:20:33.131 --> 00:20:35.500
I don't follow these
tax policies on it.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:36.835
They look at the
different issues.

00:20:36.835 --> 00:20:38.737 align:start size:88% position:13%
They look at, quite
frankly, the open border

00:20:38.737 --> 00:20:40.572
and they say, I'm
not all in on that.

00:20:40.572 --> 00:20:42.074
What in the world is that?

00:20:42.074 --> 00:20:45.177 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so they just can't follow
where Democrats are going

00:20:45.177 --> 00:20:47.813
and quite frankly,
DC Democrats continue

00:20:47.813 --> 00:20:50.048
to be able to race each
other farther to the left

00:20:50.048 --> 00:20:53.118
and there's this constant
push over and over again

00:20:53.118 --> 00:20:55.153 align:start size:88% position:13%
to see who can out-left
the next person

00:20:55.153 --> 00:20:57.522 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they're just racing away
from the American people.

00:20:57.522 --> 00:20:59.458
- Yeah. Well my follow
up to that always is

00:20:59.458 --> 00:21:00.926
do you think they believe it?

00:21:00.926 --> 00:21:02.894
When you're talking to
your Democrat colleagues,

00:21:02.894 --> 00:21:06.331 align:start size:97% position:3%
do you think that they believe
all of this stuff works?

00:21:06.331 --> 00:21:07.866
That the big spending works,

00:21:07.866 --> 00:21:09.935
that the giant government
programs work, et cetera?

00:21:09.935 --> 00:21:11.937 align:start size:88% position:13%
Or is it that they're
just in the rat race now

00:21:11.937 --> 00:21:13.171
and they can't turn back?

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:15.440
- It's a bit of both on that.

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:16.875 align:start size:81% position:19%
I do run into some
true believers

00:21:16.875 --> 00:21:18.510
that are here in the Senate

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:20.012
and I also run into
some folks saying,

00:21:20.012 --> 00:21:23.181
hey, I'll just get barbecued
if I don't go along with this,

00:21:23.181 --> 00:21:25.050 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I'm not sure
this is a great idea.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:28.587
So it just kind of depends
from person to person on it.

00:21:28.587 --> 00:21:30.822 align:start size:88% position:13%
But there is a huge push
for them to continue

00:21:30.822 --> 00:21:32.591 align:start size:94% position:6%
to go farther and
farther and farther left.

00:21:32.591 --> 00:21:35.093 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think it's the
remaking of the country.

00:21:35.093 --> 00:21:37.696 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's this celebration
of, our country was evil

00:21:37.696 --> 00:21:40.232 align:start size:88% position:13%
and its founding and
its history and its laws

00:21:40.232 --> 00:21:43.035
and so we're gonna remake
the nation in our image,

00:21:43.035 --> 00:21:44.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
into what we always wanted
it to be able to be.

00:21:44.836 --> 00:21:46.305
But she's never been-

00:21:46.305 --> 00:21:48.307 align:start size:88% position:13%
She's always been a
nation about individuals

00:21:48.307 --> 00:21:51.576 align:start size:97% position:3%
having freedom altogether
and committed to each other.

00:21:51.576 --> 00:21:53.645
And so, but that's not
where they're headed.

00:21:53.645 --> 00:21:55.580
- Senator, I got
one more for you.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:57.683
The one that I'm sure
you don't wanna answer.

00:21:57.683 --> 00:22:00.552 align:start size:94% position:6%
Republicans are gonna
have to pick a president.

00:22:00.552 --> 00:22:03.188
They're gonna have to all,
you know, follow somebody.

00:22:03.188 --> 00:22:05.691
Obviously Trump has announced,

00:22:05.691 --> 00:22:08.026
there's a feeling that
DeSantis will announce.

00:22:08.026 --> 00:22:10.228 align:start size:97% position:3%
To me, there's actually a
pretty great crop of people,

00:22:10.228 --> 00:22:11.063
I think.

00:22:11.063 --> 00:22:12.597
Nikki Haley, Tim Scott.

00:22:12.597 --> 00:22:14.399
Like there's really
a decent group there,

00:22:14.399 --> 00:22:17.169
which you don't see that kind
of bench with the Democrats.

00:22:17.169 --> 00:22:20.105
What do you see as the
future of all of this?

00:22:20.105 --> 00:22:21.273
- I'm sorry, you're breaking up.

00:22:21.273 --> 00:22:22.140
I can't hear you anymore.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:22.974
(Dave laughs)

00:22:22.974 --> 00:22:24.609
No, I'm kidding.

00:22:24.609 --> 00:22:26.611 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yeah, the party's
gonna have to decide.

00:22:26.611 --> 00:22:28.714 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean honestly, you just
listed off a bunch of folks.

00:22:28.714 --> 00:22:30.949
Mike Pence, Mike Pompeo,
they're also out there.

00:22:30.949 --> 00:22:34.219
There's a whole big long
list of great Republicans

00:22:34.219 --> 00:22:36.088
that are out there that
are good thought leaders

00:22:36.088 --> 00:22:38.490
that the nation's gonna have
to be able to make a decision

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:41.360 align:start size:84% position:16%
on which way they're
gonna go on this.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:43.295 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we'll see when the
primary's is actually begun.

00:22:43.295 --> 00:22:44.763
Obviously, it's already begun.

00:22:44.763 --> 00:22:46.998 align:start position:0%
President Trump has already
announced he's gonna run again.

00:22:46.998 --> 00:22:48.834
By the time we get
into early next year,

00:22:48.834 --> 00:22:50.969
we're really gonna
know the whole field.

00:22:50.969 --> 00:22:53.405
If somebody's not
in by this summer,

00:22:53.405 --> 00:22:55.574
they're not gonna
be able to get in.

00:22:55.574 --> 00:22:57.409
But there are a lot
of folks that I think

00:22:57.409 --> 00:22:59.244
were considering it
or looking at this

00:22:59.244 --> 00:23:00.912 align:start size:97% position:3%
to try to figure out if
they can do the fundraising

00:23:00.912 --> 00:23:02.881
and they can get out there and
be able to share the message.

00:23:02.881 --> 00:23:05.317
I've said this publicly
so I'll say it to you

00:23:05.317 --> 00:23:06.551
and to your audience.

00:23:06.551 --> 00:23:08.387
Again, in Oklahoma,
when I talk to folks,

00:23:08.387 --> 00:23:10.389 align:start position:0%
about half the folks that I
talked to that are Conservative

00:23:10.389 --> 00:23:12.023
will say they love
President Trump,

00:23:12.023 --> 00:23:14.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
they love the way he speaks,
they love his policies.

00:23:14.159 --> 00:23:15.360
They want him to run again.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:17.028
And about half the
folks will say to me,

00:23:17.028 --> 00:23:18.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
I love President Trump
and his policies,

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:20.399
I don't like him as a messenger.

00:23:20.399 --> 00:23:22.401 align:start size:97% position:3%
I wish there was someone
else that had those policies

00:23:22.401 --> 00:23:25.337
that didn't have that way of
actually speaking and acting

00:23:25.337 --> 00:23:26.805
and reacting to people.

00:23:26.805 --> 00:23:29.741 align:start size:97% position:3%
So we're pretty divided
even as a state in Oklahoma.

00:23:29.741 --> 00:23:31.810
It'll be interesting to be able
to see how many other states

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:33.245
are divided as well.

00:23:33.245 --> 00:23:35.013 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's been very strong
support for the Trump policies

00:23:35.013 --> 00:23:36.815
because we saw what he
did for immigration,

00:23:36.815 --> 00:23:38.083
we saw what he did
for the economy.

00:23:38.083 --> 00:23:40.252
We saw what he did for
our national standing

00:23:40.252 --> 00:23:41.720
on the world stage.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:44.589
So people are very grateful for
that, but they're divided on

00:23:44.589 --> 00:23:46.057
whether they're gonna gonna
keep going on that again.

00:23:46.057 --> 00:23:49.194 align:start size:94% position:6%
So again, we're gonna
solve that as Republicans

00:23:49.194 --> 00:23:50.695
to be able to put
up our nominee.

00:23:50.695 --> 00:23:53.498
What I want to have us do, is
to be able to win at the end

00:23:53.498 --> 00:23:54.633
and to be able to articulate

00:23:54.633 --> 00:23:56.334 align:start size:84% position:16%
what real Conservative
policies are.

00:23:56.334 --> 00:23:59.204 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's fascinating to me
the number of college students

00:23:59.204 --> 00:24:02.441 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I talked to nowadays
that are young Conservatives

00:24:02.441 --> 00:24:04.376
that will want to talk
to me about Ronald Reagan

00:24:04.376 --> 00:24:08.380
and they're intrigued with a
Republican who won 49 states.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:09.881
They're like, how
did that happen?

00:24:09.881 --> 00:24:12.484
How did a Republican win
49 states in an election,

00:24:12.484 --> 00:24:14.719
in his re-election even?

00:24:14.719 --> 00:24:16.221
And to be able to get that.

00:24:16.221 --> 00:24:18.924
And so they're fascinated
with that jovial, outgoing,

00:24:18.924 --> 00:24:21.026 align:start size:84% position:16%
Conservative thought
that's winsome

00:24:21.026 --> 00:24:23.028
and winning people over on that.

00:24:23.028 --> 00:24:24.262
And I get that.

00:24:24.262 --> 00:24:25.197
Culturally, we've gotta
be able to figure out

00:24:25.197 --> 00:24:26.431
what direction we're gonna go.

00:24:26.431 --> 00:24:28.066
But as a party, I
want to continue

00:24:28.066 --> 00:24:30.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
to not just burn heretics,
I wanna make converts

00:24:30.469 --> 00:24:32.737
because I think these
Conservative principles

00:24:32.737 --> 00:24:35.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
actually do work and help
people in every race,

00:24:35.874 --> 00:24:37.442
every community,
every background.

00:24:37.442 --> 00:24:39.878 align:start size:84% position:16%
It really truly
does lift all boats.

00:24:39.878 --> 00:24:42.314
And so let's go tell the message

00:24:42.314 --> 00:24:45.417
and let's some messengers
out there go duke it out.

00:24:45.417 --> 00:24:47.152 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Senator, I
appreciate your time.

00:24:47.152 --> 00:24:48.553
I hope we can do it again.

00:24:48.553 --> 00:24:51.022 align:start size:97% position:3%
Perhaps in person in
Oklahoma, because I do think

00:24:51.022 --> 00:24:52.524
even though they call
'em flyover states,

00:24:52.524 --> 00:24:56.127
they are important states
and that would be fun.

00:24:56.127 --> 00:24:58.096 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You know, I actually
land in Oklahoma

00:24:58.096 --> 00:24:59.631
so I know it's possible
to be able to land there

00:24:59.631 --> 00:25:00.899
and not just fly over it.

00:25:00.899 --> 00:25:02.334
And if we get there,

00:25:02.334 --> 00:25:04.503 align:start size:97% position:3%
I can take you to some
really great barbecue places

00:25:04.503 --> 00:25:06.905 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we can sit down
and have a great visit.

00:25:06.905 --> 00:25:08.139
- Right on. Awesome.

00:25:08.139 --> 00:25:09.774 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well I appreciate it and
thanks for your time.

00:25:09.774 --> 00:25:11.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you're looking for
more honest and thoughtful

00:25:11.443 --> 00:25:14.412
conversations about politics
instead of non-stop yelling,

00:25:14.412 --> 00:25:16.414 align:start size:84% position:16%
check out our
politics playlists.

00:25:16.414 --> 00:25:18.149 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if you wanna
watch full interviews

00:25:18.149 --> 00:25:21.052
